00:02:41.242 --> 00:02:43.254
<v Lucy Collins> Well, thank you so much for
coming along to my talk today, 

00:02:43.255 --> 00:02:47.578
where we are gonna answer the question: 
Do
accessibility personas actually work?

00:02:49.492 --> 00:02:51.049
I want to start with a bit of a moan.

00:02:51.316 --> 00:02:52.488
I promise I'll keep it brief.

00:02:52.726 --> 00:02:55.849
In my opinion, digital accessibility
has a dehumanization problem.

00:02:56.161 --> 00:02:59.364
Somewhere along, along the line, a
practice that was fundamentally about

00:02:59.386 --> 00:03:02.703
understanding how real experiences or
real people, sorry, experience the world,

00:03:02.971 --> 00:03:04.646
got reduced to a checklist and a scanner.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.320
Automated tools are fast and affordable.

00:03:07.482 --> 00:03:10.253
They give you a score and a
perceived sense of security that

00:03:10.255 --> 00:03:11.569
you're getting accessibility done.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.402
But that focus on code-level fixes
means that we often forget to ask

00:03:15.404 --> 00:03:18.665
the most important question: 
Can real people achieve the task 

00:03:18.666 --> 00:03:20.341
that they set out to do 
on your website?

00:03:20.862 --> 00:03:23.903
This means that while a service can
pass a compliance audit, 

00:03:23.904 --> 00:03:25.665
it still fails the people 
it is meant to serve.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.712
Now, a quick caveat at this point.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.180
This is not about having a go
at WCAG and the testing tools

00:03:30.192 --> 00:03:31.610
that exist on the market.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.557
For many, they are a great, great way
to begin an accessibility journey.

00:03:35.971 --> 00:03:39.071
They take something that can feel
quite overwhelming and emotionally

00:03:39.083 --> 00:03:42.126
charged and make it feel a
little bit easier to get started with.

00:03:42.509 --> 00:03:44.515
But the problem is that 
many organizations

00:03:44.516 --> 00:03:46.514
and many of the people that 
I work with over the years

00:03:46.515 --> 00:03:49.676
never progress beyond 
that checklist mentality.

00:03:49.887 --> 00:03:53.365
They see WCAG compliance 
and that score out of 100 

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:56.777
from their scanners as the end
goal, when really it's the start.

00:03:57.024 --> 00:03:58.584
It is the floor and not the ceiling.

00:03:59.247 --> 00:04:02.468
This creates a gap in the 
experiences that we create for people.

00:04:03.946 --> 00:04:06.904
So I'm calling this 
the accessibility gap,

00:04:06.905 --> 00:04:09.721
and it's a well-known fact that those 
sites that pass an automated audit,


00:04:09.722 --> 00:04:12.064
and can technically satisfy the rules

00:04:12.065 --> 00:04:14.000
still remain exhausting, confusing,
practically unusable, 

00:04:14.001 --> 00:04:16.970
something that makes you 
wanna tear your hair out,

00:04:16.971 --> 00:04:18.430
and I've seen this myself 
across the research sessions

00:04:18.431 --> 00:04:20.750
that we at my agency run.

00:04:20.772 --> 00:04:23.447
Some of the most common examples
we see from a kind of technically

00:04:23.447 --> 00:04:27.034
compliant website that are still
practically really hard for people with

00:04:27.034 --> 00:04:31.387
disabilities to use will be things like
unhelpful or verbose alt text.

00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:35.722
So that picture of a cup of tea, 

00:04:35.723 --> 00:04:40.025
you can tell I'm British, 
which just has the alt text "tea."

00:04:40.652 --> 00:04:41.340
What's that gonna do?

00:04:41.357 --> 00:04:42.585
That's not doing 
anything for everyone.

00:04:42.586 --> 00:04:46.272
But technically, that satisfies
potentially the WCAG checklist.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.501
Ditto, we've worked for some
brilliant hotel organizations 

00:04:50.502 --> 00:04:54.529
who have these amazing photos of 
their hotel rooms, gorgeous things, 

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.027
but to get all of that into an alt text, 

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:58.090
they end up with these 
lengthy, lengthy things  

00:04:58.091 --> 00:05:02.331
that perhaps are better 
communicated in other ways.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:04.746
We've also got dense and
complex paragraphs of text.

00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:08.180
We know that at the level that most
people try and achieve kind of digital

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:14.317
accessibility, so WCAG level AA, 
really, readability and jargon use

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.539
is not something that gets covered off.

00:05:15.549 --> 00:05:19.376
And as a result, we can end up with really
beautifully technically designed websites

00:05:19.668 --> 00:05:22.870
that then have a dense paragraph of text
plonked right into the middle of it,

00:05:22.883 --> 00:05:26.844
which is heavy in jargon, long sentence
length, cognitively very demanding.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.198
The same is true for
lengthy button labels.

00:05:29.221 --> 00:05:33.058
These might be hard for people to
understand, comprehend, but actually also

00:05:33.058 --> 00:05:37.441
perhaps if you've got a speech impediment
and you use voice-to-text software

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.783
software, you might struggle to actually
then activate those lengthy buttons.

00:05:40.159 --> 00:05:42.001
Technically, it passes, 
it's nice and descriptive, 

00:05:42.031 --> 00:05:44.650
but actually practically 
it's hard work to use.

00:05:45.164 --> 00:05:47.597
And the same when it comes to 
kind of the tabbing

00:05:47.598 --> 00:05:49.060
 and how we move through websites.

00:05:49.061 --> 00:05:52.510
We know that, again, we want everything
to be keyboard accessible, 

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:54.623
but if you create very lengthy 
navigational journeys, 

00:05:54.625 --> 00:05:57.089
that can be a fairly exhausting 
experience for people.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:01.337
So what we end up with then 
is something that technically works 

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:04.650
but practically doesn't work 
for the people 

00:06:04.651 --> 00:06:07.552
who really are trying to 
achieve their online goals.

00:06:07.553 --> 00:06:10.307
Obviously, the single best way to close
that accessibility gap 

00:06:10.308 --> 00:06:14.241
and to truly understand whether or not 
experience works for disabled people is, 

00:06:14.242 --> 00:06:17.197
surprise surprise, 
to work directly with disabled people.

00:06:17.513 --> 00:06:21.145
What this means is that we include people
in research, we observe real behaviors,

00:06:21.308 --> 00:06:22.832
we listen to their lived experience.

00:06:23.044 --> 00:06:26.828
We test those complete end-to-end
tasks and journeys, 

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.477
not just the interaction point 
in isolation.

00:06:28.863 --> 00:06:31.519
We involve people throughout that
process, not just shoehorning them

00:06:31.519 --> 00:06:34.669
in at the end as a kind of
check… an additional tick box.

00:06:35.063 --> 00:06:37.167
And even better, we work alongside
disabled colleagues, 

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.998
which means obviously sorting out 
our recruitment practices.

00:06:42.659 --> 00:06:47.643
This is so powerful, and the reason
is, is because we all have a 

00:06:47.643 --> 00:06:49.248
mental model about the 
way the world works.

00:06:49.888 --> 00:06:52.796
The great thing about working with
people with disabilities is

00:06:52.796 --> 00:06:55.846
that it breaks down that mental model,
or it gives us a different perspective

00:06:55.868 --> 00:06:57.623
on the way that people see the world.

00:06:58.217 --> 00:07:00.486
Our mental model is unique to us.

00:07:00.556 --> 00:07:03.619
My mental model will be unique to
me, and yours will be unique to you.

00:07:03.861 --> 00:07:07.705
It's an internal understanding of… 
that we use to predict

00:07:07.705 --> 00:07:11.025
how something is gonna work, and it
is, as I say, totally unique to you.

00:07:11.344 --> 00:07:14.369
It's shaped by our bodies and our
senses, the previous experiences that

00:07:14.369 --> 00:07:17.861
we've had in life, the education and the
upbringing that we've had, the language

00:07:17.861 --> 00:07:21.193
that we speak, or languages that we
speak, the culture we've grown up in,

00:07:21.193 --> 00:07:25.173
the technology we use, our overall
levels of confidence, and any privilege

00:07:25.176 --> 00:07:28.323
and/or exclusion that we've
experienced over the course of our life.

00:07:28.762 --> 00:07:32.128
It informs absolutely everything that
we do, how we experience the world,

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.873
and crucially for this conversation,
how we design digital experiences.

00:07:36.352 --> 00:07:39.274
A non-disabled white male developer
building a digital experience that

00:07:39.275 --> 00:07:42.724
supports people of colour with vision
loss cannot possibly understand the

00:07:42.724 --> 00:07:44.270
lived experience of those end users.

00:07:44.782 --> 00:07:48.167
The less experience we share with the
person using our product, 

00:07:48.186 --> 00:07:50.931
the more dangerous our 
untested assumptions become, 

00:07:50.932 --> 00:07:52.947
and this is where exclusion occurs, 

00:07:52.948 --> 00:07:56.460
when we solve problems using
our own biases and experiences.

00:07:57.203 --> 00:08:00.746
An important note at this point, 
it's not because designers and developers

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:04.166
perhaps don't care, it's because no
one can possibly feel experiences

00:08:04.167 --> 00:08:05.280
that they have never encountered.

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:09.380
Disability and access needs often
enlarge this experience gap, 

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.116
because the needs of someone using a
website perhaps without vision, 

00:08:12.118 --> 00:08:14.265
without fine motor control, 
without hearing, or limited cognition 

00:08:14.266 --> 00:08:17.765
is so very far removed from, normally, 

00:08:17.766 --> 00:08:19.476
from the experience of 
those people building them.

00:08:19.477 --> 00:08:22.292
The beauty about bringing
different perspectives into the

00:08:22.292 --> 00:08:27.419
room and by either working alongside disabled
people or involving them in research, 

00:08:27.421 --> 00:08:30.522
is that it challenges all sorts of things:
our assumptions, our priorities, 

00:08:30.524 --> 00:08:34.394
our language, the design conventions 
that we rely on, the definitions of 

00:08:34.395 --> 00:08:37.958
what is easy, and absolutely the definition 
of what we consider to be normal.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.268
It challenges your mental model, it
changes the questions that we ask,

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.668
and ultimately, it enables us to
build experiences that work better

00:08:44.952 --> 00:08:46.927
for a much broader range of people.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:51.415
But, and unfortunately it is
a big but, the world that we

00:08:51.416 --> 00:08:52.597
live in is far from ideal.

00:08:52.851 --> 00:08:56.524
And while I would simply love everyone
to be doing inclusive research 

00:08:56.528 --> 00:09:00.374
all the time throughout the development
process and beyond, the reality is

00:09:00.375 --> 00:09:02.996
it requires a lot of things that
many organizations don't have.

00:09:03.474 --> 00:09:05.912
The most common that we hear in
the conversations that we have

00:09:05.912 --> 00:09:10.123
with our clients are time, budget,
and access to participants.

00:09:10.511 --> 00:09:12.544
It's lack of time.

00:09:12.748 --> 00:09:14.883
When are we gonna fit this
into a development cycle?

00:09:14.911 --> 00:09:15.851
It's lack of money.

00:09:15.885 --> 00:09:18.936
We haven't put aside the money to be
able to do this on a regular basis.

00:09:19.256 --> 00:09:22.256
And it's this perceived lack of
access to the right people,

00:09:22.256 --> 00:09:24.510
although I often think that 
that one's a bit of a cop-out.

00:09:24.928 --> 00:09:29.048
It also might be a lack of accessibility
expertise or knowledge that exists 

00:09:29.048 --> 00:09:32.585
 within the team, and therefore the confidence 
 to go out to do this sort of research.

00:09:32.828 --> 00:09:34.845
It might be that you don't
have the suitable technology

00:09:34.846 --> 00:09:35.784
that allows you to do it.

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:38.689
And I think lastly, most
interestingly, it might be 

00:09:38.692 --> 00:09:40.244
a lack of organizational commitment.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:44.845
For many organizations, the desire to do
this sort of research is absolutely there.

00:09:44.889 --> 00:09:47.773
I've had conversations with clients
who said that they would love to be

00:09:47.773 --> 00:09:50.514
able to do more in the way 
of inclusive research, 

00:09:50.514 --> 00:09:52.971
but many of them don't have 
the resources available to do it.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:56.804
Think charities, small agencies,
freelancers, public sector bodies who

00:09:56.805 --> 00:09:59.781
perhaps just don't have the funds to
regularly do this type of work alongside

00:09:59.781 --> 00:10:02.986
everything else that these very
squeezed budgets are trying to cover.

00:10:03.821 --> 00:10:05.948
In contrast, and at the other
end of the scale, of course, we

00:10:05.948 --> 00:10:09.139
do have those organizations that
are just not prioritizing this,

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.514
despite all the evidence 
that tells us that 

00:10:11.517 --> 00:10:13.866
prioritizing accessibility 
is good for business.

00:10:14.495 --> 00:10:17.890
Even if you are an individual fighting
the good fight in one of these businesses,

00:10:17.891 --> 00:10:21.888
if you don't have that buy-in from senior
management, it can be a real uphill battle

00:10:21.926 --> 00:10:25.674
and really hard to get the prioritization
that this sort of research needs.

00:10:26.745 --> 00:10:30.027
So when research doesn't happen, we
find that teams typically 

00:10:30.032 --> 00:10:32.627
fall back on their own experience, 
which as we know, 

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.046
that is your mental model, 
that is unique and limited to you.

00:10:35.735 --> 00:10:39.184
We look at generic assumptions or
stereotypes, which can be very dangerous.

00:10:39.543 --> 00:10:43.246
We look at standards alone, which is why 
there is this heavy reliance on WCAG.

00:10:44.061 --> 00:10:47.527
We look at what's easiest to test and
to get out the door.

00:10:47.751 --> 00:10:50.779
Or perhaps we listen to the loudest
internal voice, the person who has

00:10:50.779 --> 00:10:54.467
the strongest opinions, who wants
the certain piece of information

00:10:54.479 --> 00:10:56.747
or interaction on the experience.

00:10:57.141 --> 00:11:00.893
Or we try designing for this
mythical average user, 

00:11:00.893 --> 00:11:02.712
the person we perceive 
that our users to be.

00:11:02.936 --> 00:11:06.898
And as a result, we forget about the
huge spectrum and variety of people 

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:08.509
that might actually be 
using our services.

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:12.623
So what tools can we use in this
constrained environment 

00:11:12.623 --> 00:11:14.449
that would help us to interrupt 
these mental models?

00:11:15.198 --> 00:11:17.850
And I think hopefully, 
given the title of this talk,

00:11:18.068 --> 00:11:21.420
it will be no surprise that where
we've been building up to is this:

00:11:22.147 --> 00:11:24.492
Personas.
Enter stage left.


00:11:24.819 --> 00:11:27.298
Personas are a fictional representation 
grounded in evidence.

00:11:27.593 --> 00:11:30.820
So what we would commonly capture
within a persona is a goal.

00:11:30.821 --> 00:11:32.393
What somebody is trying to achieve. 


00:11:32.404 --> 00:11:35.098
The behaviours: Typically, how would
they go about doing that?

00:11:35.363 --> 00:11:39.400
The motivations: What motivates them 
to engage with you in the first place?

00:11:39.817 --> 00:11:41.247
Some context and background.

00:11:41.284 --> 00:11:44.200
Okay, what do we need to know
about that that is gonna inform

00:11:44.201 --> 00:11:45.303
the way they engage with us?

00:11:45.832 --> 00:11:48.037
The frustrations that they
might have along the, the way.

00:11:48.559 --> 00:11:51.895
And importantly, personas need to
be built on data, not guesswork.

00:11:52.426 --> 00:11:55.735
When we build digital personas for
clients, we do a shed load of user

00:11:55.746 --> 00:11:59.145
research first to really understand
their target customer, and then build

00:11:59.147 --> 00:12:01.129
those personas on top of that foundation.

00:12:01.833 --> 00:12:04.619
Often personas are also given
some demographic details.

00:12:04.619 --> 00:12:07.654
No doubt people have already seen
these, where you'll have 

00:12:07.752 --> 00:12:10.744
Salesperson Sally or 
Checkout Charlie.

00:12:11.012 --> 00:12:12.475
They often alliterate for some reason.

00:12:12.477 --> 00:12:13.725
I think it makes it more memorable.

00:12:14.046 --> 00:12:17.263
So you might include a name 
and age, a bit of a backstory,

00:12:17.277 --> 00:12:20.180
which is what then differentiates 
personas from archetypes.

00:12:21.048 --> 00:12:23.942
And this personalization
is, I guess, important because 

00:12:23.946 --> 00:12:27.929
it takes advantage of our human tendency 
to be more captivated by 

00:12:27.952 --> 00:12:29.788
concrete instances than generalizations.

00:12:29.789 --> 00:12:34.092
And what I mean by that is that 
we seem to care more because

00:12:34.092 --> 00:12:37.558
we can imagine them as real people,
rather than a faceless list of criteria.

00:12:38.553 --> 00:12:41.560
So the history, very quick history
lesson around the kind of

00:12:41.574 --> 00:12:43.101
origins of personas.

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:46.717
The modern interaction design personas
that I'm referencing today, the ones

00:12:46.717 --> 00:12:50.417
that you might use to help you,
build your website or to write your

00:12:50.443 --> 00:12:54.512
marketing content, these are commonly
seen as being closely associated

00:12:54.513 --> 00:12:58.522
with a chap called Alan Cooper and his
goal-directed design work in the 1990s.

00:12:58.874 --> 00:13:01.931
So Cooper used personas to shift
software teams away from

00:13:01.932 --> 00:13:05.825
designing around technical capability
and towards user goals and behaviors.

00:13:06.214 --> 00:13:08.933
It was a really interesting time
in, in the world of the web.

00:13:09.354 --> 00:13:12.866
At that point, back in the '90s,
websites were built by technical people.

00:13:12.875 --> 00:13:14.875
That's what was required in
order to build a website

00:13:14.876 --> 00:13:16.481
because they were technical by nature.

00:13:16.787 --> 00:13:20.177
Obviously, the responsibility and
ownership of websites these days has

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:23.721
typically moved over to a marketing
team, a digital product team, and

00:13:23.722 --> 00:13:25.753
so this maybe seems less obvious.

00:13:26.007 --> 00:13:29.064
But at the time, when you were dealing
from your mental model of being a

00:13:29.064 --> 00:13:33.984
technical person, a developer, that is a
very niche, specific mental model to have.

00:13:34.189 --> 00:13:37.978
You are in the top 1% of people
when it comes to digital literacy.

00:13:38.512 --> 00:13:41.834
And so Alan Cooper and his work
basically was trying to get people to

00:13:41.834 --> 00:13:45.360
step out of that mental model of being
a very digitally literate, technical

00:13:45.363 --> 00:13:48.912
person to think about what might
those end users actually need from

00:13:48.912 --> 00:13:50.136
the experiences that we're building.

00:13:50.688 --> 00:13:54.357
And what those personas had then
helped with was to encourage teams

00:13:54.357 --> 00:13:57.666
to ask the right questions at the
right time in the design, development,

00:13:57.694 --> 00:13:59.106
and content creation journey.

00:13:59.451 --> 00:14:02.363
So these might be questions like,
would this persona understand this?

00:14:02.545 --> 00:14:03.547
What might stop them?

00:14:03.735 --> 00:14:05.081
What would they need to continue?

00:14:05.202 --> 00:14:06.718
What happens when something goes wrong?

00:14:06.928 --> 00:14:08.729
And are we designing only for ourselves?

00:14:08.843 --> 00:14:09.673
So on and so forth.

00:14:10.316 --> 00:14:12.773
They can prompt us to look at a
problem through a different lens,

00:14:13.049 --> 00:14:16.103
and this in turn can help you
see where your own biases exist.

00:14:17.015 --> 00:14:19.344
Now, these are personas kind of generally.

00:14:19.683 --> 00:14:23.201
We often see personas get used
for general customer segments,

00:14:23.582 --> 00:14:27.057
but we can also drill down to what
accessibility personas look like.

00:14:27.477 --> 00:14:28.426
And these aren't new.

00:14:28.663 --> 00:14:31.735
Accessibility personas have been around
for many years, and there's many good

00:14:31.737 --> 00:14:36.353
examples of what accessibility personas
could look like, including 

00:14:36.375 --> 00:14:40.682
the likes of the Government Digital
Service here in the UK, W3C, so

00:14:40.683 --> 00:14:42.722
the authors of WCAG, and Stanford
University, 

00:14:42.724 --> 00:14:45.155
which is the persona you can see 
on the right of the screen here.

00:14:45.918 --> 00:14:50.772
These have all been created to help 
organizations think about what works

00:14:50.775 --> 00:14:54.086
for people with different access needs
online and highlight the common barriers

00:14:54.104 --> 00:14:58.544
that designers and developers might
be inadvertently putting in the way 

00:14:58.545 --> 00:15:00.296
by the way that they design 
digital experiences.

00:15:01.089 --> 00:15:03.738
These have been created
in lots of different ways.

00:15:04.094 --> 00:15:08.690
Some will focus on disability
type, some will focus on need.

00:15:08.892 --> 00:15:12.465
Some are stories based on a single
person, some are informed by

00:15:12.471 --> 00:15:16.070
research through across kind of
multiple people with similar

00:15:16.084 --> 00:15:18.091
disability types or functional needs.

00:15:18.917 --> 00:15:22.100
The link between all of them is that
they're providing a provocation.

00:15:22.433 --> 00:15:25.495
They're providing us an opportunity
to consider a perspective that is

00:15:25.496 --> 00:15:27.220
different from our own mental model.

00:15:30.206 --> 00:15:31.483
And they can do a really good job of it.

00:15:32.187 --> 00:15:36.944
So research into accessibility personas
suggests that they can raise awareness

00:15:36.944 --> 00:15:39.899
of accessibility needs and help
teams identify accessibility issues,

00:15:40.211 --> 00:15:44.379
particularly when personas are based
on evidence and

00:15:44.394 --> 00:15:46.102
are co-created with disabled people.

00:15:46.187 --> 00:15:48.817
That last bit is very important,
and I will be coming back to it.

00:15:49.346 --> 00:15:50.799
So why can they work?

00:15:51.046 --> 00:15:53.738
I think one of the first and most
important things that personas,

00:15:53.759 --> 00:15:57.851
accessibility personas can do is that they
make unfamiliar barriers more visible.

00:15:58.259 --> 00:16:01.376
They can simply be an awareness-raising
tool, for want of a better word.

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:06.115
Many people don't realize that certain
access needs exist, not because they

00:16:06.116 --> 00:16:11.280
don't care and not because… 
but it's simply because

00:16:11.281 --> 00:16:12.808
they haven't come across it in the past.

00:16:13.259 --> 00:16:15.671
Personas might give them
that moment of enlightenment.

00:16:16.238 --> 00:16:19.363
We see this all the time with clients
when we're doing inclusive research.

00:16:19.568 --> 00:16:21.591
It might be that they've
never seen a screen reader

00:16:21.592 --> 00:16:22.978
user interact with a website.

00:16:23.007 --> 00:16:25.191
It might be that they've never
understood what it's like for

00:16:25.191 --> 00:16:28.462
someone who's colourblind to
engage with a visual design.

00:16:28.849 --> 00:16:31.866
As soon as they've witnessed that
moment, it often has this real

00:16:31.866 --> 00:16:34.401
moment of enlightenment, this
moment where they think, 

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.008
"Oh, we could potentially do better."

00:16:36.333 --> 00:16:39.656
And while that moment with a real
person would be my preferred way to

00:16:39.659 --> 00:16:42.347
give them that moment of enlightenment,
that is an option that might not

00:16:42.375 --> 00:16:45.646
always be available to everyone for
the reasons we previously discussed.

00:16:46.046 --> 00:16:50.597
A persona, however, can be a little nudge
to suggest, "Hey, have you considered

00:16:50.597 --> 00:16:52.992
that this might be a need that you're
not currently aware of?"

00:16:53.197 --> 00:16:56.574
And get you to examine your own,
existing assumptions and biases.

00:16:57.573 --> 00:17:01.080
As well as introducing kind of the
unfamiliar barriers, it can also introduce

00:17:01.092 --> 00:17:04.371
technology, assistive technology,
specifically in this context.

00:17:04.737 --> 00:17:08.843
So again, there are so many tools out
there that enable people and empower

00:17:08.846 --> 00:17:10.626
people to engage with the online world.

00:17:11.290 --> 00:17:15.069
Screen readers, screen magnifiers,
speech-to-text software, switch tools,

00:17:15.192 --> 00:17:16.978
ear switches, all sorts of things.

00:17:17.384 --> 00:17:20.930
And again, I think unless you're in this
space or you've been exposed to it, you

00:17:20.935 --> 00:17:23.621
often don't know what are the different
assistive technologies available to you.

00:17:24.006 --> 00:17:27.858
These personas can once again,
do a fantastic job of educating

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:31.580
people about the tools that are
available and as a result, from a

00:17:31.581 --> 00:17:35.135
designer and developer perspective,
what you need to be considering when

00:17:35.136 --> 00:17:36.923
you take your designs forward.

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:40.644
I think it can also connect
requirements to a task.

00:17:40.964 --> 00:17:43.329
It allows us to therefore
focus on outcome.

00:17:43.397 --> 00:17:45.806
So if we go back to what we were
saying right at the very beginning

00:17:45.999 --> 00:17:49.275
with this reduction of accessibility
down to being a checklist, we've

00:17:49.286 --> 00:17:50.863
forgotten about the outcome.

00:17:50.929 --> 00:17:52.817
Can a person achieve a task online?

00:17:53.151 --> 00:17:56.936
So instead of saying, "Change this
code on this button because it fails

00:17:56.941 --> 00:18:01.579
WCAG 2.4.4," which I don't think has
ever really excited anybody, 

00:18:01.581 --> 00:18:03.285
we can describe the 
purpose of this button.

00:18:03.592 --> 00:18:06.774
We can say we need to describe the
purpose of this button so a blind person

00:18:06.779 --> 00:18:10.664
can confidently click on it and achieve
the desired task that we have set them.

00:18:11.527 --> 00:18:13.693
It then also gives
people a shared language.

00:18:13.993 --> 00:18:17.714
There is so much fear about saying the
wrong thing in the accessibility world.

00:18:17.981 --> 00:18:21.436
Providing a shared and approved language
can take away some of that fear so

00:18:21.438 --> 00:18:25.311
people feel that they can talk about
disability, they can talk about access

00:18:25.313 --> 00:18:29.113
needs in an environment where it is
gonna then challenge the assumptions

00:18:29.143 --> 00:18:33.682
that are making us build and design
ineffective and inaccessible experiences.

00:18:34.509 --> 00:18:37.138
And finally, I think it can
encourage questions earlier and make

00:18:37.164 --> 00:18:38.800
accessibility easier to remember.

00:18:39.793 --> 00:18:43.005
Ideally, we want to be asking the
question, is it accessible, at

00:18:43.071 --> 00:18:44.767
every single step in the process.

00:18:45.591 --> 00:18:50.101
But the problem is, is that accessibility
is just one part of a much bigger puzzle.

00:18:50.414 --> 00:18:52.715
Often if you are designing and
developing, you're not

00:18:52.763 --> 00:18:55.891
only thinking about accessibility,
you're thinking about budget, 

00:18:55.912 --> 00:18:59.705
you're thinking about brand guidelines, you're
thinking about interaction design.

00:19:00.014 --> 00:19:03.085
There's all sorts of things that are 
going on in your head, and often other things

00:19:03.086 --> 00:19:06.716
take precedent over accessibility because 
it gets pushed to the bottom of the list.

00:19:07.073 --> 00:19:11.237
The wonderful thing about personas is 
when used correctly, they can provide

00:19:11.256 --> 00:19:15.747
that nudge in those moments that allows 
people to remember that they need to ask

00:19:15.758 --> 00:19:18.033
that question, "Oh, is this accessible?"

00:19:18.274 --> 00:19:21.542
And instead of leaving it right to the
very end of that process, we can make

00:19:21.543 --> 00:19:25.368
sure that we are just slowly, slowly, 
embedding that accessible first

00:19:25.374 --> 00:19:26.961
thinking into everything that we do.

00:19:28.478 --> 00:19:34.025
All that being said, there are, however,
some justifiable skepticism around

00:19:34.026 --> 00:19:37.730
accessibility personas, which I think is
very, very important for us to inspect

00:19:37.813 --> 00:19:39.492
as part of this conversation today.

00:19:40.103 --> 00:19:44.590
A badly made disability or accessibility
persona can be worse than no persona

00:19:44.595 --> 00:19:48.364
at all because it gives an assumption
the appearance of evidence, and

00:19:48.383 --> 00:19:49.616
that can be really dangerous.

00:19:50.049 --> 00:19:53.499
The problem with these sorts of personas
is that they, they can be at risk

00:19:53.499 --> 00:19:55.433
of reducing someone to a diagnosis.

00:19:55.885 --> 00:19:59.542
They might even unintentionally
or potentially even intentionally

00:19:59.547 --> 00:20:03.157
reinforce stereotypes that can be
harmful to the disabled community.

00:20:03.741 --> 00:20:07.685
They imply a single disability
experience, so if you are blind, you

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:10.874
have the same experience as every
other blind person that exists in the

00:20:10.874 --> 00:20:12.893
planet, which we know is not the case.

00:20:13.473 --> 00:20:16.096
It might overemphasize certain
assistive technologies.

00:20:16.302 --> 00:20:19.177
We often seen screen readers get
pulled out as the kind of most

00:20:19.307 --> 00:20:23.080
important of assistive technologies
because technically they can be

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:24.738
the most complex to design for.

00:20:25.116 --> 00:20:28.146
But they shouldn't get unnecessary
weight over other important assistive

00:20:28.146 --> 00:20:32.378
technologies that if not designed for
could be journey stopping for those users.

00:20:33.034 --> 00:20:36.982
It ignores context and intersectionality
and obviously all the lived experience

00:20:36.982 --> 00:20:40.153
that a disabled person brings
along with them to any experience

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:41.183
that they're having online.

00:20:41.449 --> 00:20:44.789
It's not just a person who's plopped
into a website, it is a person who

00:20:44.803 --> 00:20:48.652
comes with all the battles that
they have fought over the period of

00:20:48.656 --> 00:20:51.952
their life to break down the barriers
that society can put in their way.

00:20:52.271 --> 00:20:54.984
And so we've got to remember that
lived experience, that context,

00:20:55.011 --> 00:20:57.913
and that intersectionality exists
before they even come to our site.

00:20:58.563 --> 00:21:02.188
It can create false confidence and
at worse, they can be used in

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.268
replacement of research with real people.

00:21:04.996 --> 00:21:08.108
For me, the most dangerous thing
is that they do reduce a complex

00:21:08.135 --> 00:21:11.331
intersectional and unique lived experience 
down to a single sheet of paper.

00:21:12.032 --> 00:21:16.327
I mentioned already that a 
single disability experience 

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:17.697
is not something that 
exists in the world.

00:21:18.029 --> 00:21:20.215
There is no single 
blind user experience.

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:24.168
A person may have no vision, low vision, 
or fluctuating vision that changes

00:21:24.172 --> 00:21:25.275
throughout the course of the day.

00:21:25.718 --> 00:21:28.923
They might use a screen reader, 
magnification or both.

00:21:28.978 --> 00:21:32.642
They might be highly experienced at
using their technology or newly learning.

00:21:32.684 --> 00:21:36.144
They might use different forms of
interaction, touch, keyboard, voice,

00:21:36.144 --> 00:21:39.680
or a combination of all the above, depending on
how they're feeling or what they're doing.

00:21:40.064 --> 00:21:41.552
They might have additional access needs.

00:21:41.555 --> 00:21:45.331
We know that comorbidities 
within disability are very normal, 

00:21:45.333 --> 00:21:50.781
so they might also be dealing with 
having dyslexia or they might have, 

00:21:50.826 --> 00:21:53.965
additional physical disabilities
that are impacting them in a different

00:21:53.967 --> 00:21:58.123
way that are not being captured by
having a pure kind of blind persona.

00:21:58.948 --> 00:22:00.840
And they might be working in
radically different contexts

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:02.134
that we just are unaware of.

00:22:03.001 --> 00:22:05.657
So I think it's really important
for us to remember that a diagnosis

00:22:05.658 --> 00:22:07.003
at this point is not a behaviour.

00:22:07.069 --> 00:22:10.855
A disability type does not tell you what
someone is trying to do, how they do it,

00:22:10.869 --> 00:22:14.533
what they know, the technology they use,
the barriers and the environments,

00:22:14.551 --> 00:22:17.649
the barriers the environment creates
for them, and what lived experience

00:22:17.652 --> 00:22:18.934
they're bringing to that interaction.

00:22:19.387 --> 00:22:21.810
So we have to proceed with
care when we think about how we

00:22:21.811 --> 00:22:23.453
create accessibility personas.

00:22:24.057 --> 00:22:28.266
But that being said, I do think despite
these justifiable concerns that

00:22:28.266 --> 00:22:32.910
they can still be a useful tool if, in
capital letters, we use them correctly.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:36.127
So setting up for persona success.

00:22:36.631 --> 00:22:41.061
To create successful personas, we first
need to be really clear about what

00:22:41.128 --> 00:22:43.081
they are, and this is that big if.

00:22:43.405 --> 00:22:47.621
We need to really define
the role that personas play within

00:22:47.622 --> 00:22:50.616
our organization, within our design
and development process, and with

00:22:50.616 --> 00:22:52.316
the content creation process as well.

00:22:52.943 --> 00:22:56.812
The question is not, does this persona
perfectly represent a disabled person?

00:22:57.134 --> 00:22:57.850
Of course, it doesn't.

00:22:57.925 --> 00:23:01.254
It never could, and it never
should try, more importantly.

00:23:01.687 --> 00:23:04.539
The question is, does it reveal
a relevant barrier that the team

00:23:04.547 --> 00:23:06.124
might otherwise fail to consider?

00:23:06.751 --> 00:23:10.331
As explored, personas can help us
generate questions, challenge assumptions,

00:23:10.332 --> 00:23:14.381
explore scenarios, and, and maybe even
identify research gaps that we need

00:23:14.381 --> 00:23:17.662
to go out and fill, perhaps anticipate
barriers that people with certain

00:23:17.662 --> 00:23:21.456
disability types might experience,
and then prioritize what we take into

00:23:21.461 --> 00:23:23.385
testing with people down the line.

00:23:24.203 --> 00:23:26.189
They are a hypothesis generator.

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.080
They are not a method of testing.

00:23:28.766 --> 00:23:32.293
Finally, they can absolutely never
replace the involvement of real people.

00:23:32.335 --> 00:23:34.607
I feel I've probably made that
point a couple of times already,

00:23:34.608 --> 00:23:36.352
but it feels for me so important.

00:23:36.861 --> 00:23:39.363
So personas are a lens, not a likeness.

00:23:39.418 --> 00:23:41.054
They are there to be a provocation.

00:23:41.504 --> 00:23:43.048
They should provoke, not prove.

00:23:43.079 --> 00:23:46.469
They are a hypothesis generator,
not a validation or a testing

00:23:46.473 --> 00:23:49.731
method, and they should never
replace real people in the process.

00:23:51.241 --> 00:23:52.787
So how do we go about building them?

00:23:52.867 --> 00:23:54.195
We kind of have the principles.

00:23:54.205 --> 00:23:55.488
We know how we're framing it.

00:23:55.525 --> 00:23:57.954
We know how we're going to
use personas potentially.

00:23:58.396 --> 00:24:02.155
But if they're going to be useful, they
need to be built very, very carefully.

00:24:02.535 --> 00:24:05.064
Otherwise, they risk reinforcing
exactly the assumptions that

00:24:05.064 --> 00:24:06.029
we're trying to challenge.

00:24:06.554 --> 00:24:09.218
So these are the principles
that I believe support building

00:24:09.218 --> 00:24:11.053
effective accessibility personas.

00:24:13.428 --> 00:24:15.574
Start with outcome, not condition.

00:24:16.425 --> 00:24:19.541
My first principle, and the one that I
think is most important to begin with.

00:24:19.684 --> 00:24:22.964
So rather than beginning with a prompt
such as, "Let's create a persona who is

00:24:22.965 --> 00:24:27.231
blind," we start with a task or an outcome
of what someone is trying to achieve.

00:24:27.921 --> 00:24:30.824
Let's take a shopping
website, for example.

00:24:30.907 --> 00:24:34.732
On this shopping website, I want to
go and successfully buy a blue jumper.

00:24:35.071 --> 00:24:35.860
That is the task.

00:24:35.939 --> 00:24:37.782
Might need, not need to be
quite as specific as that.

00:24:37.829 --> 00:24:39.339
It might just be a shopping experience.

00:24:39.634 --> 00:24:43.358
Or if we're on a government website,
I want to go and find out how I

00:24:43.631 --> 00:24:46.729
apply for a new driving… 
that's probably not a good example.

00:24:46.731 --> 00:24:50.792
I wanna find out how
to submit my tax return.

00:24:50.797 --> 00:24:52.204
Let's pick something really exciting.

00:24:52.582 --> 00:24:55.529
So we start with the task that people
are trying to achieve rather than

00:24:55.711 --> 00:24:59.820
the, the, rather than the, the,
the disability that somebody has

00:24:59.852 --> 00:25:01.276
or the diagnosis that somebody has.

00:25:01.717 --> 00:25:02.765
What are they trying to do?

00:25:02.937 --> 00:25:05.348
What might prevent them from
doing that in the first place?

00:25:05.592 --> 00:25:08.805
And what assumptions has the design
made about how they will perceive

00:25:08.805 --> 00:25:12.444
the information, understand it,
navigate it, or provide input if it

00:25:12.452 --> 00:25:14.314
is some form of input is required.

00:25:14.905 --> 00:25:17.385
A diagnosis alone does not tell
us how someone will behave.

00:25:17.714 --> 00:25:19.371
It does not tell us what technology
they use, 

00:25:19.418 --> 00:25:22.456
how experienced they are
in using that technology, 

00:25:22.456 --> 00:25:23.577
or what environment they are in.

00:25:24.039 --> 00:25:27.431
By focusing on the outcome, we can design
solutions that work for people with shared

00:25:27.432 --> 00:25:28.906
needs regardless of the starting point.

00:25:29.859 --> 00:25:32.968
This means that if we design
something that works for somebody who

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:36.386
is a screen reader user, or perhaps
we're designing

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:39.177
something that works well for people
with different neurotypes or with 

00:25:39.177 --> 00:25:42.898
low vision, then actually we're probably
gonna be designing an experience that

00:25:42.898 --> 00:25:46.659
also works well for someone like me at
the end of a busy workday where I've

00:25:46.659 --> 00:25:48.079
had to make an awful lot of decisions.

00:25:48.109 --> 00:25:50.540
I now have to go home and look
after my children, 

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:51.931
which is gonna fatigue me even further.

00:25:52.318 --> 00:25:55.231
The amount of cognitive load, uh, or
the amount of cognitive processing

00:25:55.231 --> 00:25:59.184
power that I might have at my disposal
at the end of a long day like that is

00:25:59.187 --> 00:26:03.447
is gonna… I'm going to benefit
from having a much simplified process.

00:26:03.913 --> 00:26:07.464
So by flipping the focus to focus on…
sorry, by flipping the priority

00:26:07.466 --> 00:26:11.495
to focus on outcome, this means that
we're going to be able to design

00:26:11.511 --> 00:26:14.798
for that intersectionality that exists
within disability, but also more

00:26:14.798 --> 00:26:18.492
broadly when we think about situational
and temporary access requirements.

00:26:19.168 --> 00:26:22.063
This leads me nicely onto the
second principle,

00:26:22.063 --> 00:26:25.542
which is that we design on for 
focus on functional interaction.

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:29.062
So the most useful pers-
losing my words now.

00:26:29.063 --> 00:26:32.279
The most useful personas are built
around how someone interacts with that

00:26:32.281 --> 00:26:34.012
product or service as just mentioned.

00:26:34.012 --> 00:26:35.445
So can they perceive the information?

00:26:35.494 --> 00:26:36.341
Can they understand it?

00:26:36.341 --> 00:26:37.210
Can they navigate it?

00:26:37.605 --> 00:26:40.121
Can they enter information,
make decisions, but can they

00:26:40.127 --> 00:26:43.717
also recover from errors and
complete the intended outcome?

00:26:44.325 --> 00:26:47.196
I do think this is where frameworks
like the European Accessibility

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:49.884
Act's Functional Performance
Criteria can be really helpful.

00:26:50.194 --> 00:26:54.374
So for anyone who's not familiar with
these already, this is, I think, 

00:26:54.386 --> 00:26:58.135
13 different, functional performance
criteria that are research-backed

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:02.579
outcomes that if we can design digital
experiences that deliver on all of

00:27:02.579 --> 00:27:06.518
those different outcomes, then we
will be creating inclusive experiences

00:27:06.519 --> 00:27:08.364
that work for almost everybody.

00:27:09.136 --> 00:27:13.068
They're named to encourage us to think
beyond conditions, beyond disability

00:27:13.068 --> 00:27:16.596
types, and consider the human functions
that interactions design on such as,

00:27:16.781 --> 00:27:18.518
can you use this service without vision?

00:27:18.553 --> 00:27:20.209
Can you use this service without hearing?

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:21.811
Can you use this service without speech?

00:27:22.072 --> 00:27:24.124
So on and so forth down
through the criteria.

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:27.902
So this changes the question from
which disability have we represented

00:27:27.959 --> 00:27:32.311
to which human capabilities
does this experience assume?

00:27:33.085 --> 00:27:35.037
But functional need is not
the only part of the picture.

00:27:35.155 --> 00:27:37.236
A persona also needs human context.

00:27:37.605 --> 00:27:40.931
Two people with two similar access
requirements may interact very

00:27:40.931 --> 00:27:43.506
differently because of their
confidence, previous experience,

00:27:43.533 --> 00:27:47.544
environment, fatigue, responsibilities,
or the consequence of failure.

00:27:48.307 --> 00:27:51.266
So the third principle is that
we need to build from evidence.

00:27:51.821 --> 00:27:54.947
A persona may be fictional in
character, but the insight

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:56.629
behind it should not be fictional.

00:27:56.678 --> 00:27:59.716
It should be grounded in
lots of solid research.

00:27:59.966 --> 00:28:02.806
It should draw from real research,
observed behaviors, interviews,

00:28:02.814 --> 00:28:06.342
accessibility support data, or perhaps
any existing user need evidence

00:28:06.345 --> 00:28:10.062
that you might have within your
organization or through collaboration

00:28:10.082 --> 00:28:12.386
with specialist or community groups.

00:28:12.908 --> 00:28:17.350
Every detail should earn its place on
your persona by helping someone

00:28:17.379 --> 00:28:18.792
make a better digital decision.

00:28:19.365 --> 00:28:23.329
A stock image, an invented backstory,
or a list of generic symptoms does

00:28:23.330 --> 00:28:26.807
not make a persona evidence-based, and
that's where the danger really comes in.

00:28:27.301 --> 00:28:30.976
It can help to be really honest 
as you're creating personas 

00:28:30.986 --> 00:28:34.451
about what is the evidence
that we have, what is the evidence

00:28:34.451 --> 00:28:37.413
that we don't have, and what are the
gaps that as a result we need to fill.

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:39.822
So which details have
we directly observed?

00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:43.239
Which were reported
by participants, or where have

00:28:43.239 --> 00:28:44.934
we inferred that information?

00:28:45.264 --> 00:28:47.010
Which still need to be tested further?

00:28:47.415 --> 00:28:50.310
They become really dangerous, or personas
become really dangerous, and nobody

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:53.677
remembers which part came from the
research and which parts were added during

00:28:53.678 --> 00:28:57.012
the workshop when we were getting a bit
carried away and giving these personas,

00:28:57.249 --> 00:28:59.475
a kind of a real identity.

00:28:59.741 --> 00:29:03.559
So every single point on those personas,
be it the goals they're trying to achieve,

00:29:03.572 --> 00:29:07.619
the frustrations that they have, 
the motivations that bring them to

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:11.202
your site, the access needs 
that we are consider- or the functional

00:29:11.202 --> 00:29:14.552
needs, sorry, that we are considering, 
the assistive technology that

00:29:14.552 --> 00:29:18.768
they might be using to realize that
functional interaction, all of that needs

00:29:18.768 --> 00:29:21.062
to be based on research with real people.

00:29:21.424 --> 00:29:24.897
Which then to make the point again,
brings us back to this fourth and

00:29:24.897 --> 00:29:27.765
final principle that we need to involve
real people in their development.

00:29:28.259 --> 00:29:31.243
So disabled and neurodivergent
people should not only appear as

00:29:31.243 --> 00:29:32.565
the subjects of these personas.

00:29:32.919 --> 00:29:36.508
As well as conducting research with
people to inform the foundations of

00:29:36.508 --> 00:29:40.418
the personas, you might want to get
advice and feedback from personas

00:29:40.454 --> 00:29:42.290
about the final output as well.

00:29:42.545 --> 00:29:46.024
People can play a really 
meaningful role in shaping

00:29:46.024 --> 00:29:49.435
and reviewing and also challenging
what you've put in there to ensure

00:29:49.435 --> 00:29:52.678
that we haven't inadvertently added
any stereotypes, we're not missing

00:29:52.691 --> 00:29:55.025
context and assumptions.

00:29:55.540 --> 00:29:56.621
Um, sorry.

00:29:56.623 --> 00:29:59.221
We're not missing context, and that
there aren't assumptions there that

00:29:59.221 --> 00:30:02.376
may be invisible to the team creating
the persona because, again, 

00:30:02.376 --> 00:30:03.873
the lived experience 
isn't there in the group.

00:30:04.699 --> 00:30:07.347
They can also tell you whether the
persona reflects a recognizable

00:30:07.347 --> 00:30:09.941
pattern of experience, or whether
we've reduced someone to a

00:30:09.943 --> 00:30:11.579
condition or a set of barriers.

00:30:12.094 --> 00:30:14.038
That involvement should
obviously be properly recognized,

00:30:14.072 --> 00:30:15.351
and most importantly, paid.

00:30:15.873 --> 00:30:19.760
Lived experience is an expertise, and
it deserves appropriate remuneration.

00:30:20.932 --> 00:30:23.150
And even when real people have
helped create the personas, 

00:30:23.150 --> 00:30:26.381
it does not kind of then replace
the research going forward.

00:30:26.685 --> 00:30:30.419
Its role really is to help teams ask those
better questions, explore scenarios, and

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:32.112
recognize where more evidence is needed.

00:30:32.758 --> 00:30:35.844
So the test of an effective
accessibility persona is not whether

00:30:35.854 --> 00:30:39.785
it perfectly represents a group of
people or a specific person.

00:30:40.322 --> 00:30:42.270
No persona can or should try to do that.

00:30:42.609 --> 00:30:46.556
The test is whether it helps the team
notice a relevant barrier, challenge

00:30:46.556 --> 00:30:50.385
an assumption, and make better decisions
that they would that they wouldn't

00:30:50.385 --> 00:30:51.903
have made without those personas.

00:30:53.702 --> 00:30:59.188
Really, a persona is a thinking
tool, and this, if I was to re-term,

00:30:59.317 --> 00:31:01.483
a definition for personas…

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:02.121
Or re-term?

00:31:02.152 --> 00:31:03.920
That's not a very good
way of describing it.

00:31:04.123 --> 00:31:08.606
If I was to redefine what personas
were, I would call them a thinking tool.

00:31:08.817 --> 00:31:12.472
They are there to provide a provocation
that helps us ask better questions.

00:31:13.338 --> 00:31:16.453
But alone, they do not prove
that your site is accessible.

00:31:16.658 --> 00:31:21.382
So just because you have designed your
site to work for said persona, 

00:31:21.383 --> 00:31:25.380
who, whatever we've decided to call them, that
doesn't necessarily mean that we've proved

00:31:25.534 --> 00:31:27.288
that the site is therefore accessible.

00:31:27.463 --> 00:31:29.311
That is where your testing
is gonna come into it.

00:31:29.764 --> 00:31:32.500
So to run through that once again,
accessibility personas 

00:31:32.513 --> 00:31:33.784
should be grounded in lived experience.

00:31:34.038 --> 00:31:36.342
They should describe patterns,
not universal truths.

00:31:36.665 --> 00:31:38.578
They should focus on
goals and interactions.

00:31:38.843 --> 00:31:42.332
They should make limitations
explicit so teams know confidently

00:31:42.588 --> 00:31:45.055
where to use personas and
where to go looking elsewhere.

00:31:45.554 --> 00:31:47.204
They should be reviewed and updated.

00:31:47.506 --> 00:31:48.917
These are not static documents.

00:31:48.933 --> 00:31:52.692
These are documents that need to be
updated, reviewed on a regular basis,

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:54.363
at least annually, if not more.

00:31:54.798 --> 00:31:58.019
They should lead towards research, and
they should never be used as evidence

00:31:58.066 --> 00:32:00.056
of validation or proof of concept

00:32:02.852 --> 00:32:06.111
To finish things off, I feel
for me that, that it's important

00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:07.299
to explore what happens next.

00:32:07.393 --> 00:32:11.203
So great, we've talked a lot about how
do we create good, effective personas.

00:32:11.453 --> 00:32:13.769
We've talked about the principles
that would lead us down the

00:32:13.769 --> 00:32:16.447
path of creating those personas
that then work well for people.

00:32:16.803 --> 00:32:17.916
But what do you do with them?

00:32:18.107 --> 00:32:22.258
You've got perhaps five, six, seven, I
don't know, you've got as many personas

00:32:22.259 --> 00:32:26.985
as you want depending on, on what
you've been trying to capture, who you've

00:32:26.985 --> 00:32:29.206
been trying to capture in those personas.

00:32:29.601 --> 00:32:31.453
But what do you do with
them once you've got them?

00:32:31.937 --> 00:32:35.040
This, for me, and I see this all the
time with the clients that we work

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:36.447
with, is one of the biggest problems.

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:40.284
It's not necessarily how they're created,
it's what do we do with them afterwards.

00:32:40.495 --> 00:32:43.222
So you've invested time and research,
you've produced a thoughtful,

00:32:43.243 --> 00:32:44.689
beautiful set of personas.

00:32:44.895 --> 00:32:48.307
You've launched them with enthusiasm
and shared them across the business, and

00:32:48.307 --> 00:32:53.098
then they quietly sit in a folder or a
slide deck or as a page on your internet

00:32:53.129 --> 00:32:54.513
and nobody ever looks at them again.

00:32:54.888 --> 00:32:56.984
And as a result, they're
basically useless.

00:32:57.610 --> 00:33:00.672
Once this happens, they stop doing
the job that they were designed to do.

00:33:00.718 --> 00:33:03.117
They no longer challenge the
assumptions that we're, that

00:33:03.118 --> 00:33:04.431
we designed them to challenge.

00:33:04.802 --> 00:33:06.754
They are no longer
prompting better questions.

00:33:07.003 --> 00:33:10.497
They simply become another piece of
documentation that gets forgotten in

00:33:10.499 --> 00:33:15.079
the great expanse of folders
that exist within many organizations.

00:33:15.922 --> 00:33:18.428
Over the years, I've seen
organizations try all sorts of

00:33:18.428 --> 00:33:20.600
things to keep these personas alive.

00:33:20.992 --> 00:33:24.395
We worked with one client who created
cardboard cutout, full-size cardboard

00:33:24.428 --> 00:33:29.929
cutouts of people, of their
personas, who sat in their office.

00:33:30.228 --> 00:33:33.973
And so they were kind of there looking
over the shoulder of customers.

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:36.659
I've seen others who've put
them up on the office walls or

00:33:36.676 --> 00:33:38.206
introduced them into daily meetings.

00:33:38.216 --> 00:33:40.993
So as well as obviously having the
team, you might then make sure that

00:33:40.994 --> 00:33:43.723
you're bringing your personas 
into the conversation and saying, 

00:33:43.974 --> 00:33:45.416
"Okay, well, what might 
Harry think about this?

00:33:45.466 --> 00:33:49.197
Or what might Susie think about this?"
And by giving them those personalities,

00:33:49.197 --> 00:33:53.217
as we discussed previously, we can
talk about them like they are our

00:33:53.218 --> 00:33:58.783
customers, like they are people so that
we can encourage people

00:33:58.785 --> 00:34:01.904
to engage more in the conversation
rather than saying, "Oh, we need to

00:34:01.906 --> 00:34:03.580
build against this list of criteria."

00:34:04.138 --> 00:34:07.626
I've even heard of one organization
who hired actors to play the personas,

00:34:07.905 --> 00:34:11.308
and these actors would wander around
the office engaging with people,

00:34:11.317 --> 00:34:16.344
staying in character all the time, 
never, never, never, never changing

00:34:16.344 --> 00:34:19.330
from who they were supposed to be so
that they really brought them to life.

00:34:20.212 --> 00:34:23.509
And all these approaches attempt to
solve one, the same problem: 

00:34:23.511 --> 00:34:26.476
How do we make the personas present in the
moment that a decision is being made?

00:34:27.640 --> 00:34:32.627
In 2026, we have a new fun toy that might
help this, and I'm sorry to have to bring

00:34:32.781 --> 00:34:36.303
the AI words into things, but
I do think that actually this is where

00:34:36.304 --> 00:34:41.157
potentially AI could play an interesting
role when used carefully.

00:34:41.817 --> 00:34:44.771
Perhaps instead of a static document,
a persona could potentially become

00:34:44.771 --> 00:34:48.587
something a team can question, explore,
and use within a design conversation.

00:34:48.868 --> 00:34:51.171
That obviously does not
make them a real person.

00:34:51.355 --> 00:34:54.399
It does not make them a substitute
for research with real people.

00:34:54.670 --> 00:34:56.859
All of the limitations and
responsibilities that we've

00:34:56.861 --> 00:34:59.834
gone over and over as part of
this talk today still apply.

00:35:00.232 --> 00:35:04.175
But it does potentially give us a way
of interacting with these personas

00:35:04.178 --> 00:35:08.391
that we haven't previously had that
keeps them alive, it keeps them in

00:35:08.391 --> 00:35:13.549
the conversation, and it might 
make them harder to forget.

00:35:14.436 --> 00:35:17.048
The challenge is not to simply
to create better personas.

00:35:17.257 --> 00:35:19.022
It is to make sure that we are
still in the room when they are

00:35:19.022 --> 00:35:21.170
needed, or they are still in the
room, sorry, when they are needed.

00:35:21.437 --> 00:35:25.379
Challenging those mental models that we
all have that are all unique to us and

00:35:25.379 --> 00:35:29.653
provoking us to ask the really important
question: Is this accessible at the

00:35:29.654 --> 00:35:33.715
right time and frequently throughout
the design and development process?

00:35:37.191 --> 00:35:40.249
That brings me to the
end of our talk today.

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:43.228
Thank you so much for choosing
to listen to this today.

00:35:43.263 --> 00:35:44.309
I am Lucy Collins.

00:35:44.643 --> 00:35:48.836
I'm owner of Web Usability, a UK-based
user research and accessibility agency,

00:35:48.852 --> 00:35:52.159
and co-founder of CollectivAlly,
an accessibility thinking tool that

00:35:52.160 --> 00:35:55.921
helps organizations make inclusion
part of every digital decision.

00:35:56.321 --> 00:35:58.726
My details are here on the slide.

00:35:58.876 --> 00:35:59.608
Please do connect.

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:01.269
I'd love to continue the conversation.

00:36:01.653 --> 00:36:03.707
And now I think it's probably 
time for some questions.

00:36:09.061 --> 00:36:11.621
<v Danielle> Awesome.
Cool. Thank you so much.


00:36:11.654 --> 00:36:15.958
I love how approachable
that you make the personas for

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:19.689
people, as something
other than just marketing use.

00:36:20.017 --> 00:36:25.174
And changing that perspective
from checklists to behaviors.

00:36:25.494 --> 00:36:28.449
I think that's kind of 
a thing that we get so used

00:36:28.449 --> 00:36:29.769
to is using those checklists.

00:36:29.777 --> 00:36:31.486
So I really like that.

00:36:32.053 --> 00:36:33.109
<v Lucy> Good. 
Thank you.


00:36:34.012 --> 00:36:34.359
<v Danielle> Cool.

00:36:34.369 --> 00:36:40.273
So if we jump into some questions
here, how do you decide 

00:36:40.274 --> 00:36:44.843
what goes into a persona or how
many you need, making sure that

00:36:44.843 --> 00:36:49.163
it covers enough… and that
you're checking enough things, but without

00:36:49.163 --> 00:36:54.793
it just becoming this... putting it 
all into one bucket persona?

00:36:55.251 --> 00:36:55.677
<v Lucy> Yeah.

00:36:55.741 --> 00:36:59.209
I'm gonna give that really annoying
answer of, "It depends."

00:36:59.227 --> 00:36:59.442
<v Danielle> Of course.

00:36:59.460 --> 00:37:05.065
<v Lucy> because it depends on sort of your
customers, who you're looking 

00:37:05.066 --> 00:37:07.453
to represent within that group of personas.

00:37:07.752 --> 00:37:11.706
Obviously, we don't want to have so many
that people can't hold these personas

00:37:11.709 --> 00:37:14.704
in their head because, again, that's
part of the point of this, is that

00:37:14.704 --> 00:37:18.466
they need to be memorable in order to
disrupt those mental models and provide

00:37:18.466 --> 00:37:20.774
that provocation that I was describing.

00:37:21.053 --> 00:37:23.388
But that being said, you know,
particularly when we think about

00:37:23.404 --> 00:37:27.702
accessibility personas, given the 
diverse and intersectional nature

00:37:27.702 --> 00:37:31.438
of disability, we can't possibly
sort of reduce that to 

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:33.731
a single disabled persona.

00:37:33.732 --> 00:37:35.254
That's not appropriate.

00:37:35.459 --> 00:37:38.141
It's not right, and it obviously
wouldn't necessarily do the job

00:37:38.148 --> 00:37:41.510
that we need these personas to
do around that provocation piece.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:46.811
So when we think about accessibility
personas specifically,

00:37:46.825 --> 00:37:50.845
this is where I find it very useful
to lean on established research, 

00:37:50.871 --> 00:37:54.119
and evidence bases like the 
European Accessibility and 

00:37:54.122 --> 00:37:58.817
Functional Performance Criteria, also referenced in,
revised Section 508 in the US as well.

00:37:59.194 --> 00:38:04.022
Those are useful starting points where we
do consider this outcome-based persona.

00:38:04.348 --> 00:38:08.838
I think it's 13 or 14 
functional performance criteria.

00:38:08.928 --> 00:38:10.039
That's probably too many.

00:38:10.066 --> 00:38:13.523
We can't hold that many in our heads,
but there might be a way for us to sort

00:38:13.523 --> 00:38:18.187
of combine some of those needs so
that we can create a pool of

00:38:18.187 --> 00:38:21.495
personas, six, seven, eight, something
like that, that is still workable.

00:38:22.111 --> 00:38:25.762
If you don't mind, I might actually
just pick up on a question that was in

00:38:25.764 --> 00:38:28.536
the Q&amp;A which I think ties in really
nice to this, and it's a really

00:38:28.543 --> 00:38:33.110
brilliant question from Biljana,
who was asking about the idea of what…

00:38:33.171 --> 00:38:37.016
You know, is it okay, like…
In fact, I can read out some of it.

00:38:37.048 --> 00:38:39.420
"How do companies
perceive accessibility personas?

00:38:39.744 --> 00:38:42.173
I have a problem with one
accessibility persona which can

00:38:42.232 --> 00:38:44.615
cover all disabilities," which
I would totally agree with.

00:38:45.050 --> 00:38:48.979
"I would prefer that normal, 
in inverted commas, personas cover 

00:38:48.979 --> 00:38:51.908
one limitation each, not necessarily
because this persona is disabled, 

00:38:51.908 --> 00:38:55.114
but because we should start looking at how
we build digital products with WCAG

00:38:55.137 --> 00:38:59.981
in mind as being normal and not for
just disabled people, which is 

00:38:59.984 --> 00:39:03.160
discrimination, and most companies
excuse themselves by disabled

00:39:03.186 --> 00:39:04.751
people not being their target market."

00:39:05.103 --> 00:39:09.406
I completely agree with that sentiment
and, you know, what I would love is for

00:39:09.407 --> 00:39:14.301
us to be building access requirements
into personas more generally.

00:39:14.719 --> 00:39:17.932
That being said, I know from the work
that we've done with our clients is

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:23.332
that because accessibility isn't baked
into the way that a lot of organizations

00:39:23.332 --> 00:39:28.207
work, it's still something that happens
at the end in the kinda QA testing-bit

00:39:28.535 --> 00:39:30.743
of that design and development process.

00:39:31.013 --> 00:39:34.707
We're needing to be kind of more
forcibly interrupting the mental

00:39:34.712 --> 00:39:38.011
models and the decision-making
processes that happen upstream

00:39:38.056 --> 00:39:40.379
earlier in those, in those processes.

00:39:40.756 --> 00:39:45.390
So while I would love, and I would
completely agree with the sentiment

00:39:45.408 --> 00:39:49.542
of trying to ensure that we are
representing disabled people in our

00:39:49.544 --> 00:39:53.830
kind of personas more generally, I
think where accessibility personas

00:39:53.832 --> 00:39:57.908
specifically play a role is in
providing that kind of more forceful

00:39:57.910 --> 00:40:02.029
disruption of thought and assumption, 
particularly in organizations

00:40:02.031 --> 00:40:05.087
who are lower in or earlier in their
accessibility journey, and perhaps

00:40:05.104 --> 00:40:07.457
lower on their accessibility maturity.

00:40:07.995 --> 00:40:09.162
Um, so yeah.

00:40:09.218 --> 00:40:12.319
Sorry, I just thought I'd pick that one up
'cause it seemed like a nice continuation.

00:40:13.239 --> 00:40:13.593
<v Danielle> Yeah.

00:40:13.593 --> 00:40:15.236
That's kind of where
I was going with it.

00:40:15.252 --> 00:40:19.319
I was sort of encompassing
some of those points.

00:40:19.694 --> 00:40:25.712
So when you're starting, would
you... I know that you mention

00:40:25.723 --> 00:40:31.024
starting from a behavior or something that
someone's trying to do, but how…

00:40:31.424 --> 00:40:35.232
What does that look like besides... 
like they wanna check out?

00:40:35.245 --> 00:40:40.225
I know it's like choosing a specific
action, but how do you bring the,

00:40:40.234 --> 00:40:46.250
the data and the action together 
as sort of a first step?

00:40:46.294 --> 00:40:47.727
What does that look like?

00:40:48.183 --> 00:40:50.144
<v Lucy> So normally the way we'd
start something like this 

00:40:50.234 --> 00:40:51.606
is with user research.

00:40:51.627 --> 00:40:55.393
We'd be doing deep discovery, 
one-to-one interviews with people

00:40:55.399 --> 00:40:58.484
to understand what is it that they
want to achieve on your website,

00:40:58.484 --> 00:41:01.678
and obviously that will be unique to
your site depending on the product

00:41:01.716 --> 00:41:03.375
you offer, the service you provide.

00:41:03.707 --> 00:41:06.321
So we'll end up with kind of a
long list of requirements, 

00:41:06.323 --> 00:41:08.004
which will be general to everybody.

00:41:08.337 --> 00:41:11.930
And then we might have some additional
kind of accessibility needs in there that

00:41:11.955 --> 00:41:15.515
are specific to people who have access
requirements, people with disabilities.

00:41:15.877 --> 00:41:21.247
So we end up with this long, long list
of goals, and then you can do

00:41:21.247 --> 00:41:25.594
some additional quantitative
research practices, so perhaps something

00:41:25.596 --> 00:41:30.937
called a top task survey, a
prioritization survey where effectively

00:41:30.939 --> 00:41:35.707
we work out what are the most important
goals, and how does that vary across

00:41:35.709 --> 00:41:40.026
the different user types that we have,
both generally and also when we consider

00:41:40.026 --> 00:41:41.515
those accessibility considerations.

00:41:41.808 --> 00:41:46.296
And what that ends up giving us is
normally a sense of some

00:41:46.296 --> 00:41:48.212
top tasks and then some tiny tasks.

00:41:48.448 --> 00:41:51.602
So the top tasks are the kinda key
objectives, the key things that somebody

00:41:51.602 --> 00:41:53.399
wants to achieve on your website.

00:41:53.408 --> 00:41:55.689
So for me, I run an agency.

00:41:55.792 --> 00:41:59.136
I know that a top task for a client
coming to my agency is to find out do we

00:41:59.138 --> 00:42:01.973
deliver the service that they're looking
for, and how do I get in touch with you?

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:06.198
Those are two key journeys I'm going
to want to ensure that our customers

00:42:06.198 --> 00:42:10.446
can achieve easily, seamlessly, and
without any sort of accessibility barrier.

00:42:11.355 --> 00:42:14.774
Once you've got a sense of that
prioritization, you can effectively build

00:42:14.774 --> 00:42:19.555
out a bit of a matrix which has got your 
long old list of tasks prioritized by…

00:42:19.578 --> 00:42:22.863
from that research, and then along the
top you'll have your different customer

00:42:22.863 --> 00:42:25.855
groups depending on who they are, and
in this case, kind of the different

00:42:25.868 --> 00:42:29.143
accessibility outcomes, the accessibility
needs that we might be considering.

00:42:29.520 --> 00:42:33.705
That then normally allows us to start
grouping shared needs, because

00:42:33.707 --> 00:42:37.462
what we might have is not lots and
lots of distinct personas with a

00:42:37.462 --> 00:42:40.872
single need, but we might end up with
groups of customers who have shared

00:42:40.874 --> 00:42:44.394
needs, and therefore they
can be a single persona themselves.

00:42:44.707 --> 00:42:48.759
And that's where we can end up
simplifying the number

00:42:48.828 --> 00:42:51.571
or reducing the number of personas that
we have so that we're not trying to

00:42:51.580 --> 00:42:56.151
hold 15, 20 different persona types in
our heads, which as I said previously,

00:42:56.152 --> 00:42:59.957
kind of then makes them really hard work
and, and almost a bit pointless as well.

00:43:00.321 --> 00:43:03.299
And from there, once you start, once you
know the need, once you know the outcome

00:43:03.301 --> 00:43:06.518
we're building toward, that's when you can
start to kind of add, you know, the nice

00:43:06.518 --> 00:43:10.133
fluffy details around them that make them 
memorable, the demographic details,

00:43:10.133 --> 00:43:14.381
the backstories, the things that 
help us as human beings hold these ideas

00:43:14.392 --> 00:43:18.294
in our heads because they're not 
an abstract list of requirements.

00:43:18.294 --> 00:43:21.243
They, they have been given
a little bit of an identity.

00:43:23.006 --> 00:43:26.127
<v Danielle> Yeah, I think that that
encompasses it really well.

00:43:26.225 --> 00:43:30.841
I think it's always hard to know 
how to get started with a thing.

00:43:30.852 --> 00:43:34.075
You're like, "That sounds great. 
I wanna do that." But it's really nice

00:43:34.075 --> 00:43:36.689
to put it into those 
concrete, you know-

00:43:36.700 --> 00:43:36.841
<v Lucy> Yeah

00:43:36.841 --> 00:43:38.078
<v Danielle> ways. I think...

00:43:38.078 --> 00:43:38.794
<v Lucy> What do we actually do?

00:43:39.473 --> 00:43:40.174
<v Danielle> Yeah.

00:43:40.743 --> 00:43:45.700
I think, it... and we're also
talking in an ideal world, right?

00:43:45.700 --> 00:43:48.211
You're able to do that
research and find the people.

00:43:48.249 --> 00:43:56.127
Is there a way that you can do
some of this without... 

00:43:56.127 --> 00:43:57.910
can you incorporate that 
at a later time?

00:43:57.964 --> 00:44:01.296
Is there a general way that 
you can start to think about

00:44:01.296 --> 00:44:07.991
that bef- and then maybe as a
proof to a superior or

00:44:08.115 --> 00:44:08.125
<v Lucy> Mm-hmm

00:44:08.125 --> 00:44:10.904
<v Danielle> until you have the budget to
be able to do that as you're starting

00:44:10.906 --> 00:44:14.893
to think about those things are
there, like, sources where that you can

00:44:14.893 --> 00:44:19.770
go to find maybe popular data-

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:19.788
<v Lucy> Yeah

00:44:19.788 --> 00:44:20.250
… <v Danielle> for that?

00:44:20.297 --> 00:44:20.629
<v Lucy> Yeah.

00:44:20.966 --> 00:44:24.368
Yeah, and I think that's, again, the
nice thing as well when we think about

00:44:24.406 --> 00:44:27.895
one of these accessibility personas
specifically is I mentioned some within

00:44:27.895 --> 00:44:31.721
the presentation already, there are
established personas out there that

00:44:31.792 --> 00:44:33.383
perhaps could be your starting point.

00:44:33.386 --> 00:44:33.401
<v Danielle> Mm-hmm.

00:44:33.721 --> 00:44:36.961
<v Lucy> And then what you want to be
doing is overlaying what you know

00:44:36.962 --> 00:44:42.453
about your specific customers on those
accessibility personas which

00:44:42.533 --> 00:44:47.500
might be a way to kind of move you
along that development process.

00:44:47.502 --> 00:44:50.393
As you say, get proof of concept
so then you hopefully you can get

00:44:50.395 --> 00:44:52.084
buy-in to then do some research.

00:44:52.397 --> 00:44:55.100
Again, I wouldn't consider this
a kind of validation exercise.

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:59.196
I'd still want to be open to the
hypothesis that for your customer

00:44:59.196 --> 00:45:00.491
group there might be different needs.

00:45:00.524 --> 00:45:03.843
But yeah, to your point, for those
people that don't necessarily have lots

00:45:03.843 --> 00:45:07.691
of money for a nice meaty discovery
research project at the start of a

00:45:07.691 --> 00:45:11.745
development piece, this could help
them take that first step.

00:45:11.747 --> 00:45:13.147
So I mentioned some examples.

00:45:13.191 --> 00:45:17.058
In the UK we've got
the Government Digital Service.

00:45:17.102 --> 00:45:20.856
They have some accessibility personas that
were developed a little while ago now.

00:45:20.888 --> 00:45:23.727
But I think they're still relevant.

00:45:24.026 --> 00:45:29.334
And then similarly, W3C, they have
their own accessibility persona, 

00:45:29.372 --> 00:45:30.966
or they kinda call them user stories.

00:45:31.339 --> 00:45:34.397
And then at the final one, the
final example, I shared was that

00:45:34.401 --> 00:45:35.738
Stanford University one as well.

00:45:35.741 --> 00:45:39.674
So these are all obviously
respected organizations who I hope

00:45:39.826 --> 00:45:43.229
have done their research before
I'm here kind of promoting them.

00:45:43.421 --> 00:45:46.980
But it seems to me that they've 
given people a good place

00:45:46.984 --> 00:45:49.256
to start when you're not even 
sure where to begin with 

00:45:49.256 --> 00:45:52.249
 what an accessibility persona 
 should look like.

00:45:52.263 --> 00:45:55.238
What are the different outcomes,
different needs I should be considering?

00:45:55.435 --> 00:45:59.627
How do I make this, the number of personas
broad enough to be representative,

00:45:59.879 --> 00:46:02.118
but slim enough to be memorable?

00:46:02.319 --> 00:46:05.046
Because there is a 
balance to be found.

00:46:05.109 --> 00:46:09.044
And it can feel quite overwhelming 
at the beginning of this process.

00:46:10.134 --> 00:46:10.393
<v Danielle> Yeah.

00:46:10.393 --> 00:46:14.218
But I think it's helpful
to even hear you say

00:46:14.511 --> 00:46:15.904
five to eight or whatever.

00:46:15.906 --> 00:46:15.933
<v Lucy> Yeah.

00:46:15.933 --> 00:46:19.705
<v Danielle> Like, that sounds at first
like a lot, but also not enough.

00:46:19.734 --> 00:46:23.857
And so you're kind of like
they're… You just sort of 

00:46:23.886 --> 00:46:26.899
throw it all in a document and
then do some organizing afterwards.

00:46:26.899 --> 00:46:26.913
<v Lucy> Yeah.

00:46:26.913 --> 00:46:28.225
<v Danielle> I like that matrix idea.

